## 2. Security Annotation Comments

Use standardized inline comments that auditors can grep for.

### Security Comment Prefixes

| Prefix | Use For | Example |
|--------|---------|---------|
| `// SECURITY:` | Security-sensitive logic | `// SECURITY: Rate limit to prevent brute force` |
| `// AUTH:` | Authentication/authorization | `// AUTH: Verify user owns this resource` |
| `// AUDIT:` | Audit logging | `// AUDIT: Log failed login attempt` |
| `// RLS:` | Row-level security notes | `// RLS: Policy ensures foundry isolation` |
| `// VALIDATION:` | Input validation | `// VALIDATION: Sanitize HTML to prevent XSS` |
| `// SENSITIVE:` | Handling sensitive data | `// SENSITIVE: PII - do not log` |

### Examples

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Clear security annotations

export async function deleteFoundryMember(foundryId: string, memberId: string) {
  // AUTH: Only foundry admins can remove members
  const currentUser = await getCurrentUser()
  const isAdmin = await checkFoundryRole(currentUser.id, foundryId, 'admin')
  if (!isAdmin) {
    // AUDIT: Log unauthorized deletion attempt
    await logAuditEvent('member_delete_unauthorized', {
      foundryId,
      targetMemberId: memberId,
      attemptedBy: currentUser.id,
    })
    throw new UnauthorizedError('Only admins can remove members')
  }

  // SECURITY: Prevent self-removal of last admin
  const adminCount = await countFoundryAdmins(foundryId)
  if (adminCount === 1 && memberId === currentUser.id) {
    throw new BusinessRuleError('Cannot remove the last admin')
  }

  // RLS: Additional server-side check (RLS policies also enforce this)
  await verifyFoundryMembership(foundryId, memberId)

  // SENSITIVE: Member data - ensure cascade delete of personal data
  await removeFoundryMember(foundryId, memberId)

  // AUDIT: Log successful member removal
  await logAuditEvent('member_removed', {
    foundryId,
    removedMemberId: memberId,
    removedBy: currentUser.id,
  })
}

// ❌ WRONG - No security annotations
export async function deleteFoundryMember(foundryId: string, memberId: string) {
  const currentUser = await getCurrentUser()
  // Auditor has no idea what security checks are happening
  if (await checkRole(currentUser.id, foundryId) !== 'admin') {
    throw new Error('Not allowed')
  }
  await db.delete(members).where(eq(members.id, memberId))
}
```

### Multi-line Security Blocks

For complex security logic, use block comments:

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Block comment for complex security logic
/*
 * SECURITY: Payment Authorization Flow
 * 
 * 1. Verify the user is authenticated
 * 2. Verify the user owns the payment method
 * 3. Verify the amount doesn't exceed daily limit
 * 4. Verify the merchant is approved
 * 5. Check for fraud indicators
 * 
 * Any failure should log to audit and return generic error
 * (don't leak which check failed to potential attackers)
 */
async function authorizePayment(params: PaymentParams) {
  // Implementation
}
```

## 3. Business Logic Comments

Explain the **WHY**, not the **WHAT**. Code shows what happens; comments explain why.

### Good Business Logic Comments

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Explains WHY

// Tasks older than 90 days are archived to improve query performance
// and reduce storage costs. Archived tasks remain accessible via
// the archive view but don't appear in active task lists.
const ARCHIVE_THRESHOLD_DAYS = 90

// Founding members get a 6-month grace period before billing starts.
// This was a launch promotion (see PRODUCT-123). New foundries after
// Jan 2026 follow standard 14-day trial.
if (foundry.createdAt < FOUNDING_CUTOFF && !foundry.billingStarted) {
  return { trialDaysRemaining: calculateGracePeriod(foundry.createdAt) }
}

// Cap batch size at 50 to prevent memory issues with large foundries.
// Tested with foundries up to 10k members - 50 is the sweet spot
// between performance and memory usage.
const BATCH_SIZE = 50

// ❌ WRONG - States the obvious (what), not the why

// Set archive days to 90
const ARCHIVE_THRESHOLD_DAYS = 90

// Check if trial
if (foundry.createdAt < FOUNDING_CUTOFF) {
  // return trial days
}

// Use batch size of 50
const BATCH_SIZE = 50
```

### Reference Tickets/Requirements

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - References decisions and requirements

// PRODUCT-456: Users requested ability to snooze tasks. Max snooze
// is 30 days to prevent task abandonment.
const MAX_SNOOZE_DAYS = 30

// COMPLIANCE-89: GDPR requires user data export within 30 days.
// We target 24 hours for good UX. Job runs hourly.
const DATA_EXPORT_TARGET_HOURS = 24

// INCIDENT-2024-03-15: Removed eager loading of member avatars
// after it caused timeout for large foundries (5000+ members)
const members = await db.query.foundryMembers.findMany({
  where: eq(foundryMembers.foundryId, foundryId),
  // Avatar loaded separately on demand - see INCIDENT-2024-03-15
})
```

### Non-Obvious Decisions

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Explains non-obvious decisions

// We use <= instead of < because timezones can cause the task's
// due date to appear "in the past" by a few hours even on the due day
if (task.dueDate <= endOfDay(today)) {
  // ...
}

// Intentionally not using transaction here. If notification fails,
// we still want the task update to succeed. Notification retry
// handled by separate job.
await updateTask(taskId, updates)
await sendNotification(task.assigneeId, 'task_updated') // Fire and forget

// Sort by created_at DESC, then id DESC to ensure deterministic
// ordering even when tasks are created in the same millisecond
// (common during bulk imports)
orderBy: [desc(tasks.createdAt), desc(tasks.id)]
```

## 4. Component Documentation

### React Component Template

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Fully documented component

/**
 * TaskCard - Displays a single task with status, assignee, and actions.
 * 
 * @description Renders a task card with visual status indicators,
 * assignee avatar, due date, and quick actions (complete, snooze, delete).
 * Used in task lists, kanban boards, and search results.
 * 
 * @component
 * 
 * @example
 * // Basic usage
 * <TaskCard task={task} onComplete={handleComplete} />
 * 
 * @example
 * // With all callbacks
 * <TaskCard
 *   task={task}
 *   onComplete={handleComplete}
 *   onSnooze={handleSnooze}
 *   onDelete={handleDelete}
 *   showFoundry={true}
 * />
 */

interface TaskCardProps {
  /** The task data to display */
  task: Task
  /** Called when user marks task complete. Omit to hide complete button. */
  onComplete?: (taskId: string) => void
  /** Called when user snoozes task. Omit to hide snooze button. */
  onSnooze?: (taskId: string, until: Date) => void
  /** Called when user deletes task. Omit to hide delete button. */
  onDelete?: (taskId: string) => void
  /** Whether to show the foundry name (useful in cross-foundry views) */
  showFoundry?: boolean
  /** Additional CSS classes */
  className?: string
}

export function TaskCard({
  task,
  onComplete,
  onSnooze,
  onDelete,
  showFoundry = false,
  className,
}: TaskCardProps) {
  // State: Track loading states for async actions
  const [isCompleting, setIsCompleting] = useState(false)
  
  // ... implementation
}

// ❌ WRONG - No component documentation
interface TaskCardProps {
  task: Task
  onComplete?: (taskId: string) => void
}

export function TaskCard({ task, onComplete }: TaskCardProps) {
  // Auditor has no idea how this component should be used
}
```

### Props Interface Documentation

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Each prop documented

interface DataTableProps<T> {
  /** Array of data rows to display */
  data: T[]
  /** Column definitions including header, accessor, and render function */
  columns: ColumnDef<T>[]
  /** Called when user clicks a row. Omit to disable row click. */
  onRowClick?: (row: T) => void
  /** Enable multi-row selection. Defaults to false. */
  selectable?: boolean
  /** Called when selection changes. Required if selectable is true. */
  onSelectionChange?: (selectedRows: T[]) => void
  /** Number of rows per page. Defaults to 20. */
  pageSize?: number
  /** Show loading skeleton. Use during initial data fetch. */
  isLoading?: boolean
  /** Message to show when data array is empty */
  emptyMessage?: string
}

// ❌ WRONG - No prop documentation
interface DataTableProps<T> {
  data: T[]
  columns: ColumnDef<T>[]
  onRowClick?: (row: T) => void
  selectable?: boolean
  onSelectionChange?: (selectedRows: T[]) => void
  pageSize?: number
  isLoading?: boolean
  emptyMessage?: string
}
```

## 5. File Headers

For complex files (>200 lines, security-critical, or multi-dependency), add a file header.

### File Header Template

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Complete file header

/**
 * @file payment-processor.ts
 * 
 * @description Handles all payment processing logic including
 * creating charges, managing subscriptions, and processing refunds.
 * Integrates with Stripe for payment operations.
 * 
 * @dependencies
 * - Stripe API (stripe npm package)
 * - Database (payment_intents, subscriptions tables)
 * - Audit logging (logAuditEvent)
 * 
 * @security
 * - All payment operations require authentication
 * - Webhook endpoints verify Stripe signatures
 * - PCI compliance: card data never touches our servers
 * - All payment events logged to audit table
 * 
 * @related
 * - src/app/api/webhooks/stripe/route.ts - Webhook handler
 * - src/actions/billing.ts - User-facing billing actions
 * - supabase/migrations/20240101_payments.sql - Schema
 * 
 * @audit All operations log to payment_audit table
 */

// ❌ WRONG - No file header for complex 500-line payment file
import { Stripe } from 'stripe'
// ... 500 lines of undocumented payment code
```

### When to Add File Headers

| File Type | Header Required? |
|-----------|-----------------|
| API routes handling auth/payments | Yes |
| Files > 200 lines | Yes |
| Security-critical logic | Yes |
| Database migrations | Yes (in SQL comment) |
| Simple utility functions | No |
| React components < 100 lines | No |
| Type definition files | No |

## 6. What NOT to Comment

### Avoid These Anti-Patterns

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Don't state the obvious

// Increment counter
counter++

// Check if user is null
if (user === null) {

// Loop through tasks
for (const task of tasks) {

// Create a new date
const now = new Date()

// ✅ CORRECT - Only comment non-obvious code

// Use UTC to avoid timezone issues in scheduled jobs
const now = new Date()

// ❌ WRONG - Outdated comments

// TODO: Remove this after Q1 2024 (we're in 2026!)
// HACK: Temporary fix (been here 2 years)
// FIXME: This is broken (still broken, still no fix)

// ✅ CORRECT - If it's a known issue, file a ticket

// See TECH-DEBT-123 for planned refactor

// ❌ WRONG - Commented-out code

// const oldImplementation = () => {
//   // 50 lines of dead code
// }

// ✅ CORRECT - Delete dead code, use git history if needed

// ❌ WRONG - Explaining language features

// Use arrow function for lexical this
const handler = () => {}

// Destructure props
const { name, email } = user

// ✅ CORRECT - Explain business decisions, not syntax
```

### The Comment Decision Tree

1. Is this a security/auth check? → Add `// AUTH:` or `// SECURITY:` comment
2. Does this log to audit tables? → Add `// AUDIT:` comment
3. Is the "why" non-obvious? → Add explanation comment
4. Is this a business rule? → Add comment with context
5. Is there a ticket/requirement? → Reference it
6. None of the above? → Probably don't need a comment

## 7. Database Migration Documentation

```sql
-- ✅ CORRECT - Documented migration

/**
 * Migration: Add task_comments table
 * 
 * Purpose: Enable threaded comments on tasks for team collaboration.
 * Comments support @mentions which trigger notifications.
 * 
 * Security:
 * - RLS policy ensures users can only see comments in their foundries
 * - Users can only edit/delete their own comments
 * - Admins can delete any comment in their foundry
 * 
 * Related:
 * - Frontend: src/components/tasks/task-comments.tsx
 * - Action: src/actions/comments.ts
 * 
 * Rollback: DROP TABLE task_comments CASCADE
 */

CREATE TABLE task_comments (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  task_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES tasks(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  author_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id),
  content TEXT NOT NULL,
  -- Soft delete to preserve audit trail
  deleted_at TIMESTAMPTZ,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
  updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW()
);

-- RLS: Users can only view comments in foundries they belong to
CREATE POLICY "View comments in own foundries" ON task_comments
  FOR SELECT USING (
    EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM tasks t
      JOIN foundry_members fm ON fm.foundry_id = t.foundry_id
      WHERE t.id = task_comments.task_id
      AND fm.user_id = auth.uid()
    )
  );
```

## 8. Pre-Commit Documentation Checklist

Before committing any code, verify:

### Exported Functions
- [ ] All exported functions have JSDoc with `@description`
- [ ] All parameters documented with `@param`
- [ ] Return type documented with `@returns`
- [ ] Error conditions documented with `@throws`
- [ ] Security-sensitive functions have `@security` tag
- [ ] Audit-logged functions have `@audit` tag

### Security Comments
- [ ] Auth checks marked with `// AUTH:`
- [ ] Security logic marked with `// SECURITY:`
- [ ] Audit logging marked with `// AUDIT:`
- [ ] RLS notes marked with `// RLS:`
- [ ] Input validation marked with `// VALIDATION:`
- [ ] Sensitive data handling marked with `// SENSITIVE:`

### Business Logic
- [ ] Non-obvious decisions have comments explaining WHY
- [ ] Business rules reference tickets/requirements
- [ ] Magic numbers have explanatory comments

### Components
- [ ] React components have JSDoc with `@description` and `@example`
- [ ] Props interfaces have per-prop documentation

### Code Quality
- [ ] No commented-out code (delete it)
- [ ] No TODO/FIXME without ticket reference
- [ ] No obvious/redundant comments
- [ ] No outdated comments

## Grep Commands for Auditors

Auditors can use these commands to find security-relevant code:

```bash
# Find all security-sensitive code
rg "// SECURITY:" --type ts --type tsx

# Find all authentication checks
rg "// AUTH:" --type ts --type tsx

# Find all audit logging
rg "// AUDIT:" --type ts --type tsx

# Find all RLS-related notes
rg "// RLS:" --type ts --type tsx

# Find all input validation
rg "// VALIDATION:" --type ts --type tsx

# Find all sensitive data handling
rg "// SENSITIVE:" --type ts --type tsx

# Find functions with security considerations
rg "@security" --type ts --type tsx

# Find functions that log to audit tables
rg "@audit" --type ts --type tsx

# Find all permission checks
rg "requirePermission|checkPermission|hasPermission" --type ts --type tsx
```
